meta {
  name: 05 - PDF Extractor - Invalid Payload (Error Case)
  type: http
  seq: 5
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/runs/wait
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "assistant_id": "pdf_extractor",
    "input": {
      "pdf_base64": ""
    }
  }
}

vars:pre-request {
  baseUrl: http://localhost:2024
}

docs {
  Worth testing on purpose: what does this API do with bad/empty input?
  
  src/agent/pdf_extraction.py is written to never raise - a decode/parse
  failure comes back as a normal 200 response with the `error` field set,
  not an HTTP error. Verified response for this exact payload:
  
    {
      "pdf_base64": "",
      "text": "",
      "pages": [],
      "page_count": 0,
      "ocr_page_count": 0,
      "embedded_text_page_count": 0,
      "warnings": [],
      "error": "Could not open file: not a valid PDF"
    }
  
  This is the kind of thing that's easy to miss testing only through
  LangGraph Studio's UI, but jumps out immediately when you inspect the raw
  HTTP response in an API client.
}
